Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or nearby dwarfs; apparent magnitude alone is not definitive without distance but can give hints combined with distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; combined with Vmag, this helps derive absolute magnitude, which is key to distinguish giants from dwarfs.
- e_Plx (parallax error): Large errors reduce confidence in distance and thus in absolute magnitude estimation.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants often have certain color ranges depending on spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): Luminosity class is embedded in spectral type (e.g., III for giants, V for dwarfs). Spectral types ending with III, III/IV, or IV usually indicate giant or subgiant stars.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Most reliable feature; giants have brighter absolute magnitudes (lower or negative values) compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.

